  "summary": "On 2 August 216 BCE, during the Second Punic War, Hannibal's Carthaginian and allied army surrounded and crushed a larger Roman army near Cannae in Apulia, southeastern Italy. Ancient writers give very high Roman death tolls, while modern historians put them nearer 30,000. The battle became a model of the double-envelopment tactic.",
